    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n`:connect ssh://` https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/39378 hides its bootstrap behind progress messages. Each step runs `ssh … sh -s` with captured stdout/stderr, and Lua interprets what comes back:\n\n- The install step's output (download, extraction, errors) is seen only on failure, re-presented as `Installation failed: <stderr>`.\n- `uname` output is parsed in Lua (`get_system_info()`) to pick the release.\n- Host-key confirmation is automated (`StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new`), we use `BatchMode=yes` to avoid complexity/security risks involved with collecting/sending a password.\n\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nShow the command's own output and act on its exit code. This is simpler and presumably more reliable / less \"magic\".\n\nLeverage #40463 to run the bootstrap steps in a terminal buffer, then connect:\n\n- Run the install script via `jobstart(argv, { term = true, stdin = 'fd' })` (or `vim._core.run_in_terminal.run(argv, { feed = script })`). The user sees the real output, and success is the exit code (no parsing/scraping).\n- Fold `uname` detection into the install script. No roundtrip, no parsing in Lua.\n- Let ssh prompt in that terminal: the user answers the host-key prompt, so the `ssh -G` probe and `accept-new` go away. \n    - ? Replace `BatchMode=yes` (which suppresses the host-key prompt) with `-o PasswordAuthentication=no -o KbdInteractiveAuthentication=no`, which still disallows passwords. This allows support for key passphrases, prompted by ssh on the pty.\n\n",
